The figure shows an instance of over-repair, where Gemini-2.0-flash generates a correct patch for bug (Math-57) under the M/M format while simultaneously introducing new buggy lines. When executed on the test suite, this patch triggers two failing tests. Specifically, the LLM introduces a new variable cumulativeSum, which is accumulated with dx2[i], thereby altering the original assignment logic. It then rewrites the if condition, causing the erroneous variable to further affect the semantics of the control flow. Finally, a critical error occurs when the LLM replaces the exit statement, directly impacting the update of resultSet and subsequently the return value of the function. We find that LLMs generate fewer correct repairs under the M/M format than under the M/M format, primarily due to over-repair. These failure cases indicate that generating long-sequence patches exposes LLMs to the risk of over-reasoning.
